K	SysMain	svc	19045	SysMain (Superfetch)	Preloads programs you use often into memory, from how you have used the PC. Microsoft's IoT Enterprise services page describes it only as "Maintains and improves system performance over time".	Whether turning it off helps or hurts depends on the workload, and Hadron has no measurement either way (reasoning: it preloads for your usage pattern, so cold launches can get slower): test it.	Restart three times each way, same drivers; time cold launches of your usual apps; capture frame times with PresentMon or CapFrameX over the same game scene; keep whichever is better.			4			memory preloading; contested, test it	0	0	published	Microsoft's IoT Enterprise services page (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/iot/iot-enterprise/optimize/services) gives the description quoted above (Windows 10/11 IoT Enterprise). How it decides what to preload is not stated there; the summary is reasoning.	reasoning	Microsoft sources disagree (see Contested): Microsoft's VDI optimization tool (Windows 10 20H2+ virtual desktops) (https://github.com/The-Virtual-Desktop-Team/Virtual-Desktop-Optimization-Tool/blob/main/2009/ConfigurationFiles/Services.json) disables it for virtual desktops. Hadron puts it in Minimum because Windows boots, networks and runs games without it (reasoning); whether that helps is not known: test it. Microsoft's Windows Server 2016 service guidance (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/security/windows-services/security-guidelines-for-disabling-system-services-in-windows-server) rates it "No guidance" (Windows Server 2016), which Hadron reads as keep the default.	2/0	published	Third-party tables: wintips.org's "Default Startup Type of Services on Windows 10 PRO OS" (third-party table; no build stated, its upload date only in its URL, Office installed) (https://www.wintips.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/DefaultServicesWin10.pdf) (Windows 10 Pro, no build stated) lists "Automatic"; TweakHound's "Windows 10 Default Services v20H2" (third-party table; a default install with VirtualBox Guest Additions, so a VM) (https://tweakhound.com/?p=18808) (Windows 10 20H2, build 19042.685) lists "Automatic". The two third-party tables agree. Neither table is a capture of the release Hadron targets (TweakHound states 20H2; wintips states no build, and its URL dates its upload before that release), so the value names no build; Hadron uses it as the Windows 10 stock value.		Microsoft's IoT Enterprise services page (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/iot/iot-enterprise/optimize/services) rates SysMain "Don't disable" (Windows 10/11 IoT Enterprise); Microsoft's VDI optimization tool (Windows 10 20H2+ virtual desktops) (https://github.com/The-Virtual-Desktop-Team/Virtual-Desktop-Optimization-Tool/blob/main/2009/ConfigurationFiles/Services.json) disables it for virtual desktops and Microsoft's Windows 10 2004 VDI guide (archived) (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/remote/remote-desktop-services/rds-vdi-recommendations-2004) says to evaluate it in each environment.		table
